-- Passage rg12-argument-evidence-p04
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('e1f4f60d-1821-5754-943f-946a14678e33', 'rg12-argument-evidence-p04', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Let Teenagers Play More Than One Sport$q$, $q$Every spring, I meet parents who want their thirteen-year-old to choose one sport and train for it all year. They fear that a child who splits time between soccer, swimming and track will fall behind. After twenty years of coaching, I believe the opposite: high school athletes should play at least two sports, and clubs should stop pressuring young players to specialize.

The first reason is safety. When the Brennard Sports Medicine Clinic surveyed six hundred athletes between the ages of thirteen and eighteen, it found that those who played a single sport year round suffered overuse injuries, such as stress fractures and tendon strain, at 1.7 times the rate of those who played several. Repeating the same motions month after month gives growing bodies no chance to recover.

The second reason is success. Parents worry that variety will cost their children a college roster spot, yet a survey of athletes at Ostrava State University found that seven of every ten had played more than one sport in high school.

I have also watched variety pay off on my own teams. Dario Okonjo ran cross-country for me in the fall and played basketball in the winter. The footwork he learned on the court made him one of the smoothest runners I ever coached, and he went on to compete in college.

Finally, athletes who play several sports enjoy them more and are less likely to quit before graduation.

Young athletes have years to find the sport they love most. Parents and coaches should give them room to explore before asking them to choose.$q$, 'draft')
