-- Passage rg12-argument-evidence-p06
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('09d51f2c-d3a8-5f97-bdbb-771951c33afd', 'rg12-argument-evidence-p06', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$What the Plate Study Did and Did Not Show$q$, $q$Last spring, researchers at Westmere College tested a simple idea in one of the campus dining halls. For twelve weeks, the hall alternated between its usual eleven-inch plates and smaller nine-inch plates, switching every other week. Staff weighed the food left on returned plates at the end of each meal.

During the small-plate weeks, the weight of discarded food fell by 22 percent. The researchers argued that diners tend to fill whatever plate they are handed, and that much uneaten food comes from serving more than a person wants. A smaller plate, they reasoned, limits the first serving and therefore limits waste.

The design of the study helped the argument. Because large and small plates alternated week by week, changes in season, exam schedules or weather were spread across both conditions rather than falling on just one.

The researchers also considered an obvious objection: perhaps students simply returned for second helpings, piling up the same amount of food in two trips. To check, staff counted trips to the serving line. Return trips rose by only 6 percent in small-plate weeks, far too little to offset the drop in waste.

The findings soon traveled beyond campus, though not always accurately. A regional newsletter ran the headline Small Plates Cure Overeating. The study, however, weighed food that students threw away, not food that they ate. It did not track anyone's diet, weight or health.

The Westmere team made a careful case about waste. Readers who stretch it into a claim about eating habits are adding a conclusion that the evidence was never designed to support.$q$, 'draft')
