-- Passage rg12-argument-evidence-p08
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('0418763f-6853-5233-9321-066a2c197ede', 'rg12-argument-evidence-p08', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'read-g12-argument-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Can Volunteers Count Birds?$q$, $q$Each winter, thousands of volunteers across the Marrow Lake region spend a weekend counting the birds they see and sending their tallies to the regional wildlife office. Some scientists dismiss these counts as too unreliable for serious research. That judgment is too sweeping. Volunteer counts are trustworthy for common species, though they need professional support for rare ones.

The strongest test came three winters ago, when the wildlife office sent trained biologists to survey the same twenty sites that volunteers had covered that weekend. For twelve common species, such as chickadees and mallards, the volunteer totals fell within 10 percent of the professional totals at nearly every site.

The picture changed for rare birds. Volunteers missed roughly 30 percent of the rare species that biologists recorded, often because those birds are shy, quiet or easily confused with common relatives.

The sheer number of volunteers also helps. Any one observer may miscount, but when thousands of people report, an observer who counts too high in one place is likely to be balanced by another who counts too low somewhere else. Across the whole region, these individual errors tend to cancel out.

The practical lesson is not to choose between volunteers and professionals but to combine them. Volunteer data can track the rise and fall of common birds across a wide area, while trained biologists focus their limited time on the rare species that volunteers tend to miss.

Dismissing volunteer counts wastes a valuable resource. Trusting them blindly would be a mistake as well. Used for the right questions, they give scientists a view of the region that no small team could ever achieve alone.$q$, 'draft')
on conflict (id) do update set title = excluded.title, body = excluded.body
